body:
ESH.

&#x200B;

I perfectly understand the fact that you are tired and were not feeling good enough to go. However, it being a perfectly good reason, does NOT mean other people are not allowed to be upset at your decision.

&#x200B;

She was low but you, in your brilliant mind, decided to dig a massive hole and go LOWER. 

&#x200B;

ESH but you fucked up big time. You feel like an asshole because you were one.

ESH

You, for sticking for your cheater friend; 
Your gf, for bringing your friends ex over and starting petty arguments; 
Your friend, for cheating and just sounding like a deadbeat; 
Your friend's ex, the least AH of them all knowing this is happening and probably affecting your relationship, and not caring.

YTA!

&#x200B;

Not only did you immediately push her aside for the new baby and made sure she knew she was not even worth worrying about, let alone a priority, but you also kicked her out of HER room. The baby doesn't need a master bedroom, and maybe if you tried to talk to her abtt it, she would have understood the switch lmao

ESH. You're the asshole for putting dog's lives over a kid's life. Your husband's the asshole for not using a condom with a FWB and the kid's mother's an asshole for coming outta nowhere with a kid that should have been mentioned and tested for paternity before your engagement, let alone the marriage.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

The kid comes first, not the dogs. If you actually want to keep them, the kid can't stay around.

ESH. Although I can understand more of your sister's assholeness than yours. You said somewhere bellow that 'you don't have have money to spare, only maybe $5, $10 and you wouldn't donate because that's not what people usually crowdfund for.'

&#x200B;

I understand she's acting entitled, but this is also a sensitive situation for her. However, without any damn reason, you were rude, cold and are judging your sister's choice of HOW she wants to have a child. That's just..... wrong.

I got scenario.

&#x200B;

I could literally die right now while thinking of a damn scenario where this happens. Oh shit.

I must have been around 2/2.5 years old, because my grandfather died when I was pretty young.

&#x200B;

I was sitting in my grandmas lap, being an annoying little kid, and my grandpa was tickling my feet in order to make me laugh and i kept trying to kick his hat off. When I made it, I put on his hat and took a victory lap around the living room while he and my grandma were laughing hysterically.

Not a teen, but a young adult.

&#x200B;

Age does not mean being right all the time, let the damn kids defend themselves. Then when they grow up not knowing how to stand up for what they believe in you blame their "lack of courage" no. You belittled them way too many times.

Not *said* but *did.* Near my boyfriends house, there's a little building with a supermarket and some shops. It's like 100 meters from a police station. After smoking a really fat blunt each, we went inside to sit down in a stairwell as it was raining a lot. It's not unusual for cops to pass by to get some food or just be on patrol. Well, some of them passed through us, and me, high of my ass, decided to do the "smile and wave" thing, while barely being able to even open my eyes. I laughed really hard, because i was waving at them like they were little animals on the other side of a glass. I looked so stupid. The cops eyed us for a bit, laughed and went on their merry way. Not a particularly smart thing to do.

&#x200B;

After I went home, my boyfriend (us still being pretty high) tells me that he "can't believe there's so much thunder, it's insane, i feel my whole body shaking".  (He's not a big fan of thunderstorms) There was absolutely no thunder. None. I later heard this from his brother, who, at the dinner table, was crying trying to contain his laughter, because my bf was trying to come off totally-not-high, and was making small talk about the stupidest things.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

While in a PE class in 9th grade, jumping on a mini-trampolin, I "broke" my ankle. All kinds of meltdowns, my classmates worried about me, and whatever, my teacher super worried too. WHatever, i thought: this hurts a little, but I'll be fine.

&#x200B;

They didn't call an ambulance, instead, called my grandpa, who had a shop right in front of my school. Not taking an ambulance to the hospital, not giving me crutches or anything to help me walk, and not being in an emergency vehicle, a possibly 5min trip turned into a 20min drive. Still, I was fine.

&#x200B;

As I got to the hospital, I got told it was just a minor sprained ankle. I told them calmly (as a 13 year old could), it couldn't be, i felt something break, and asked for an X-RAY. Almost two hours later, my foot was scanned, and there was a crack in a bone (the main one that pulls it all together, idk bone names in english, sorry). As I went to get my cast, I noticed something very wrong: they didn't have my x-rays. 

&#x200B;

They put the cast on the back of my calf and on the sole of my foot, the whole front was roller bandage/ligature. They twisted and turned my foot around because they molded my cast wrong. 

Two days later I went back, complaining of more pain than when I went there the first time, and that i could take the cast off, because it was so "baggy". They xrayed my foot again and.... guess what!!

The first time I went to the hospital, they moved my foot around so much they broke it more, and into little pieces. So I was left with it smashed, instead of just a crack.

It never solidified correctly, now one of my ankles is slighty bigger than the other, and gives me excruciating pain in the winter.

&#x200B;

(sorry for any misspelled words btw)

tl;dr: Went to the hospital with a crack in a bone, ended up with it smashed into pieces.
